      "id": "vc-codex-seeded-v2-fintech-lending-fastapi-fp-002",
      "is_vulnerable": false,
      "vulnerability_class": "sql_injection_false_positive",
      "primary_cwe": "CWE-89",
      "acceptable_cwes": [
        "CWE-89",
        "CWE-943"
      ],
      "file": "backend/app/main.py",
      "location": {
        "start_line": 536,
        "end_line": 536,
        "function": "list_applications"
      },
      "severity": "high",
      "expected_category": "injection",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles SQL injection: the user-controlled query param `q` is interpolated into an f-string `f\"%{q}%\"` that feeds a WHERE clause, which a scanner sees as raw string concatenation into SQL. It is actually safe: the f-string only builds the LIKE *pattern value*, which is passed to SQLAlchemy's `BorrowerProfile.business_name.ilike(...)` / `LoanApplication.purpose.ilike(...)` ORM expressions. SQLAlchemy binds the pattern as a parameter, so no SQL is ever assembled from user input. The surrounding statement is built entirely with the typed `select()` Core/ORM construct (no `text()`, no `.execute(raw)`)."
      },
      "false_positive_trap": true,
      "campaign_id": "v2-campaign-1",
      "provider_run": "codex",
      "app": "fintech-lending-fastapi"
    },
    {
      "id": "vc-codex-seeded-v2-fintech-lending-fastapi-fp-003",
      "is_vulnerable": false,
      "vulnerability_class": "idor_false_positive",
      "primary_cwe": "CWE-639",
      "acceptable_cwes": [
        "CWE-639",
        "CWE-284",
        "CWE-862",
        "CWE-285"
      ],
      "file": "backend/app/main.py",
      "location": {
        "start_line": 305,
        "end_line": 308,
        "function": "load_app"
      },
      "severity": "high",
      "expected_category": "auth",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles an IDOR / broken-access-control sink: a caller-supplied integer `app_id` is passed straight to `db.get(LoanApplication, app_id)` to fetch a record by primary key, which scanners flag as direct object access. It is actually safe here: immediately after the fetch the code enforces `can_view_app(user, app_obj)` and raises HTTP 404 if the requesting user is not authorized, so ownership/role scope is checked before the object is returned. This is the central, correctly-guarded loader (distinct from the get_application detail path flagged elsewhere)."
      },
      "false_positive_trap": true,
